{"attack":true,"case":"The target's cross-city explanation is under-specified: it derives 'larger gap' from a 'higher storage-to-cooling ratio' but never defines the contrast class for the ratio, so the same variables (e.g., higher thermal mass, more impervious area) can imply either a larger or smaller UHI gap depending on whether they are compared to the city's own rural background or to other cities.\nThe problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the night-time gap is larger in SOME cities than in OTHERS. The target answers with a composite 'storage-to-cooling ratio' and says cities with a higher ratio develop larger gaps. But it never states what the ratio is normalized against. Its own desert-city/forest-city example only explains why a city's gap differs from its surrounding countryside (urban-vs-rural contrast), not why one city's gap is larger than another city's gap. For a cross-city claim, the relevant stored heat is the urban-minus-rural storage difference, and the relevant cooling capacity is the urban-minus-rural cooling difference; if the target's stated variables (thermal mass, impervious area, sky view, wind speed) are meant as absolute urban values, a city with much higher thermal mass but also much higher wind speed could have a lower net ratio, making the predicted direction ambiguous. Because the target does not specify the ratio's components as differentials, the cross-city prediction is not actually pinned down.\ncites: 9d2ca67b2aafb6739ad6173f79887811c98d6370a2ac4cb501f634696a24b0c4","counterexample":[{"city_pair":"Desert city A vs. temperate city B","ratio_ambiguity":"If the target's ratio uses absolute urban cooling capacity, city A has very low latent cooling (desert) and can have low wind, yielding a high storage-to-cooling ratio and a predicted large gap, matching reality. But if the ratio is the urban-minus-rural storage difference divided by urban-minus-rural cooling, city A's rural desert also stores heat and has little latent cooling, so the urban-rural storage and cooling differences are both small, giving a low ratio and predicting a small gap — the opposite of the observed large desert UHI. The target never says which ratio it means, so its cross-city prediction is internally ambiguous.","uhi_gap_A":"Large (~6-8 K)","uhi_gap_B":"Moderate (~3-5 K)"}],"premise":"The target's 'storage-to-cooling ratio' is never defined as an urban-rural differential; it is described via absolute urban traits (greater impervious area, higher thermal mass, reduced evapotranspiration, lower wind speed, lower sky view), which determine a city's own heat balance but do not by themselves determine whether its nocturnal UHI gap is larger than another city's.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"9d2ca67b2aafb6739ad6173f79887811c98d6370a2ac4cb501f634696a24b0c4","quote":"Cities with a higher storage-to-cooling ratio — arising from greater impervious area, higher thermal mass, reduced evapotranspiration, lower wind speed, and lower sky view — develop larger nocturnal UHI gaps."},{"block":"9d2ca67b2aafb6739ad6173f79887811c98d6370a2ac4cb501f634696a24b0c4","quote":"The magnitude of the nocturnal gap at any hour after sunset is proportional to the accumulated difference in cooling rates, which is set by the ratio of the urban heat storage reservoir to the total nocturnal cooling capacity."}]}